{
 "map_info": {
  "name": "Rotation and Composition",
  "lookup_name": "Trans_Rotation",
  "description": "Rotation as non-commutative composition. Applying the same operations in different orders produces divergent outcomes, revealing that spatial logic depends on sequence as much as on action. Gallery: rotation makes the hole, and the hole is the way. Three holes the width of a turning plank, crossed only when it turns - a quarter, a half, forty-five; three more where cubes roll and never pause, one a turntable you cross while it turns; three slots crossed carried and turned a quarter, the other way, half round; a hole in a plateau with the screw standing in it, up and once around; and a slot down through it, turned half round on the way. Mario cubes stand where the holes lead.",
